\section{Phase-Unwrapping Issue}
variables:
\noindent
$h$: height;
$h_{\mathrm{obs}}$: exported height;
$h_{\mathrm{corr}}$: corrected height;
$\lambda$: wavelength;
$\Delta n = n_{\mathrm{water}}-n_{\mathrm{air}}$: refractive-index difference;
$\phi$: unwrapped phase;
$\phi_{\mathrm{w}}$: wrapped phase;
$k \in \mathbb{Z}$: phase-unwrapping integer;
$q = \lambda/\Delta n$: height change per $2\pi$ phase cycle;
$m \in \mathbb{Z}$: integer-cycle height correction;


\paragraph{DHM measurement and height reconstruction.}
The camera records an intensity image of an interference pattern
(hologram). Software reconstructs the optical phase, which is known
only modulo $2\pi$. For transmission through water in air,
\[
h=\frac{\lambda}{2\pi\Delta n}\phi,
\qquad
\Delta n=n_{\mathrm{water}}-n_{\mathrm{air}},
\qquad
\phi=\phi_{\mathrm{w}}+2\pi k,\quad k\in\mathbb{Z}.
\]
Unwrapping determines the integer field $k$. One incorrect cycle
produces a height offset
\[
q=\frac{\lambda}{\Delta n}\approx 2\,\mu\mathrm{m}.
\]
This estimate assumes $\lambda\approx660\,\mathrm{nm}$ and
$\Delta n\approx0.33$; the exact export conversion remains unconfirmed.

Local unwrapping becomes ambiguous when the true height difference
between adjacent samples exceeds $q/2$. For example, with
$q=2\,\mu\mathrm{m}$, increments of $+1.4\,\mu\mathrm{m}$ and
$-0.6\,\mu\mathrm{m}$ yield the same wrapped phase difference.

\paragraph{Repair from exported height fields.}
Assuming integer-cycle errors and a known conversion, recover
wrapped phase from the unfiltered heights:
\[
\phi_{\mathrm{w}}
=\operatorname{Arg}\!\left[
\exp\!\left(i\frac{2\pi h_{\mathrm{obs}}}{q}\right)
\right].
\]
A constant reference offset can be ignored. This recovers wrapped
phase, not the original hologram. Each wrapped phase corresponds
to possible heights separated by $q$:
\[
h=\frac{q}{2\pi}\phi_{\mathrm{w}}+qk,
\qquad k\in\mathbb{Z}.
\]
Repair therefore, means choosing the correct integer branch at each
pixel, equivalently, finding an integer correction field $m$:
\[
h_{\mathrm{corr}}=h_{\mathrm{obs}}+qm,
\qquad m(x,y,t)\in\mathbb{Z}.
\]
Possible approaches:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Spatial or joint space--time unwrapping assuming smoothness among neighbors (skimage.unwrap phase). The problem is that once the phase field is generated, it does not use information from the height field anymore. It might change pixels, which have been correct in the original height field. Especially for high gradients, this can happen 
    \item Optimize directly for the height field. Assign the integer correction to every pixel. Penalize nonzero corrections to avoid correct pixels being changed. Also request nearest integer cycle adjustment for very large discontinuity. 
\end{itemize}



Problems:
- Algorithm should not alter correct pixels
- Algorithms tend to smooth out 
- Algorithms rely on some reference frame, which is hard to find bc of bursts like discontinuities.
- unknown parameters



\section{Neural SDE: A First Comparison}

Let $\bm z_n$ denote the reduced state and
$\bm v_n=\bm z_n-\bm z_{n-1}$ its increments. Using the Euler Maruyama stochastic integrator, the state model and increment model can be distinguished :

SDE state model:
\[
\bm z_{n+1}
 = \bm z_n + \bm h \bm f_\theta(\bm z_n)
 +   \sqrt{\bm h} \bm G_\theta(\bm z_n)\bm\xi_n   \quad \text{where} \quad \bm\xi_n\sim\mathcal N(\bm 0,\bm I) \quad \text{h: timestep}.
\]


SDE increment model (second-order model for the state):
\[
\bm v_{n+1}
 = \bm v_n + \bm h \bm f_\theta(\bm z_n,\bm v_n)
 + \sqrt{\bm h} \bm G_\theta(\bm z_n,\bm v_n)\bm\xi_n,
\qquad
\bm z_{n+1}=\bm z_n+\bm v_{n+1}  \quad \text{where} \quad \bm\xi_n\sim\mathcal N(\bm 0,\bm I)  \quad \text{h: timestep}.
\]


\paragraph{Drift parameterizations.}

\begin{itemize}
\setlength{\itemsep}{0.2em}

\item \textbf{Direct neural drift.}
The drift is represented by an unrestricted feed-forward network,
$\bm f_\theta(\bm z_n,\bm v_n)$. This is flexible inside the
training distribution, but if during rollout trajectories leave the training distribution, it has no notion of damping/restoring and can diverge.

\item \textbf{Damped-residual drift.}
A simple way to enforce some notion of restoring / damping in the increment model is
\[
\bm f_\theta(\bm z,\bm v)
 =-\bm K\bm z-\bm D\bm v
 +\bm r_\theta(\bm z,\bm v),
\qquad
\bm K,\bm D\succeq0.
\]
Here, $\bm K$ provides a restoring force and $\bm D$ provides damping. They
are parameterized either as positive diagonal matrices or as SPSD (via Cholesky). The neural residual $\bm r_\theta(\bm z,\bm v)$ captures nonlinear effects not described by the structured terms. There is no theoretical stability guarantee, but inductive bias.  

\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Diffusion parameterizations.}

For an SDE diffusion factor $\bm G_\theta$, the associated covariance is
\[
\bm A_\theta(\bm z,\bm v)
 =\bm G_\theta(\bm z,\bm v)
  \bm G_\theta(\bm z,\bm v)^\top
 \succeq0.
\]
Thus, $\bm G_\theta$ itself need not be symmetric or positive definite.
The Gaussian likelihood used here requires a nonsingular covariance. There are different options to parametrize the diffusion coefficient

\begin{itemize}
\setlength{\itemsep}{0.2em}

\item \textbf{Constant diagonal} 

\item \textbf{Constant full covariance}


\item \textbf{State-dependent diagonal}
    This can lead to instabilities when rollout leaves the training distribution and diffusion might amplify, which can lead to divergence. This can be mitigated by enforcing a bounded diffusion.


\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Multistep likelihood.}

Training only the one-step transition can produce small systematic errors that
accumulate during long autoregressive rollouts. Multistep training therefore
adds likelihood terms at selected rollout horizons. Here, the multistep
transition density is approximated with a Monte Carlo Gaussian mixture
(Pedersen, 1995; Dietrich et al., Chapter 3C).

Even when the Euler--Maruyama one-step transition is Gaussian, the multistep
transition generally is not, because the intermediate states are random and
the dynamics are state dependent. Starting from the same initial condition,
$M$ particles are rolled out for $h-1$ steps. Each particle then defines a
Gaussian distribution for the final endpoint velocity, giving
\[
\widehat p_\theta^{(h)}(\bm v)
 =
 \frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=1}^{M}
 \mathcal N\!\left(
   \bm v;\bm\mu_h^{(m)},\bm\Sigma_h^{(m)}
 \right).
\]
The training objective combines the one-step negative log likelihood with
weighted multistep negative log likelihoods,
\[
\mathcal L
=\mathcal L_{\mathrm{1step}}
+\lambda_{\mathrm{ms}}\sum_{h\in\mathcal H}\mathcal L_{\mathrm{ms},h}.
\]
At long horizons, estimating a high-dimensional mixture likelihood is noisy
and expensive. The increment-correlation analysis showed that higher POD modes
decorrelate faster. The likelihood dimension is therefore reduced with the
horizon: the leading energetic POD modes are always retained, while additional
modes are included only while their training-data increment correlations remain
relevant. This reduces both the number of evaluated coordinates and the number
of particles required at long horizons. In the 0p20 case this improved runtime,
long-rollout energy, and modal amplitudes. A subsequent endpoint-mean
fine-tuning step further reduced the remaining long-rollout POD mean bias.


\paragraph{First comparison.}

\begin{itemize}
\setlength{\itemsep}{0.15em}

\item Experiment: 0p20.
\item Shared POD basis with rank $r=20$.
\item trained on native timestep of 8.68 microseconds
\item 50\,Hz temporal high-pass filtering of the spatial mean.
\item 12/2/2 training/validation/test repetition split.
\item Two hidden layers with 64 neurons each.
\item SiLU activation functions.
\item Batch size of 256.
\item Training for 5 epochs.
\item Upper 5\% increment trimming.
\item Evaluation using 50{,}000-step rollouts. Five initial conditions are
chosen and five stochastic trajectories are generated from each, yielding 25
generated trajectories and five corresponding reference trajectories.

\end{itemize}

The table should be read as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\setlength{\itemsep}{0.15em}
\item The reference is the measured trajectory reconstructed in the shared
rank-20 POD space, rather than the full experimental field.
\item PSDs use the untrimmed reference. Their high-frequency content can
therefore include discontinuities excluded from model training.
\item Energy and POD statistics compare untrimmed generated trajectories with
the reference retained by the checkpoint's 5\% training-increment trim rule.
Generated trajectories are never trimmed.
\item POD mean error is the root-mean-square modal mean bias divided by each
reference mode's standard deviation; its ideal value is 0.
\item POD variance ratio is
$\operatorname{tr}(\bm\Sigma_{\mathrm{gen}})/
\operatorname{tr}(\bm\Sigma_{\mathrm{ref}})$; its ideal value is 1.
\item POD decorrelation reports generated/reference frame counts. For every
POD mode, it is the first start of 100 consecutive lags with
$|\rho|<0.1$; the table gives the median across the 20 modes. All models use
their first 5{,}001 frames, and similar generated and reference counts are ideal.
\item All Neural-SDE rows in the table use the validation split.
\item Stochastic OpInf uses its 5{,}001-frame training rollout because its
50{,}000-frame rollout diverges.
\end{itemize}


